= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

An <antiderivative> of $\sin x$ is $-\cos x$. The <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin x\,dx=[-\cos x]_0^{\pi/2}=1.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Set $u=3-2x$, so $du=-2dx$. This <integration by substitution> gives
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{dx}{3-2x}=-\frac12\ln|3-2x|+C.}
$$
The <antiderivative> is taken on either <interval> separated by $x=3/2$. Differentiating verifies the sign and factor $-1/2$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiating the <hyperbolic cosine> with the <chain rule> gives $g'(x)=3\cosh^2x\sinh x$. For real $x$, $\cosh x>0$, so the only zero of the <derivative> is $\sinh x=0$, namely $x=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{x_1=0,\qquad g(x_1)=1.}
$$
Since $\cosh x\geq1$, this <stationary point> is the <global minimum>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x>0$, <logarithmic differentiation> gives
$$
\ln f(x)=\frac{\ln x}{x},\qquad
\frac{f'(x)}{f(x)}=\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}.
$$
Since $f(x)>0$, the <derivative> vanishes precisely when $\ln x=1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{x_0=e,\qquad f(x_0)=e^{1/e}.}
$$
The <derivative> is positive before $e$ and negative after $e$, so this is a <global maximum> on the given positive domain.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The distance from the origin is $r=4\pi-t$. The direction angle, measured counterclockwise from the positive horizontal axis, is $\theta=\pi/2-t$ modulo $2\pi$. Thus the path is an <Archimedean spiral>, turning clockwise through two complete revolutions as its <radius> decreases linearly from $4\pi$ to zero.

It starts at $(0,4\pi)$, reaches $(7\pi/2,0)$ at $t=\pi/2$, crosses $(0,-3\pi)$ at $t=\pi$, returns to the positive vertical axis at $(0,2\pi)$ when $t=2\pi$, and ends at the origin when $t=4\pi$. These crossings fix both the scale and direction of the sketch.

\Image[paper-1-inward-spiral.png]
{title=Two clockwise inward turns of the spiral, with time-direction arrows and selected axis crossings}

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At the stated point, the radial distance is $7\pi/2$, so $4\pi-t=7\pi/2$ and $t=\pi/2$. Differentiate the <parametric curve>:
$$
\dot x=-\sin t+(4\pi-t)\cos t,\qquad
\dot y=-\cos t-(4\pi-t)\sin t.
$$
At $t=\pi/2$, these are $\dot x=-1$ and $\dot y=-7\pi/2$. Since $\dot x\ne0$, the <tangent line> has finite <slope>
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{\dot y}{\dot x}=\frac{7\pi}{2}.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Let the <equilateral triangle> have side <length> $a$. Its altitude is $H=\sqrt3a/2$, and its <incircle> has <radius> $r=H/3=a/(2\sqrt3)$: the <centre> lies on a median, one third of the altitude above the base. The <area> of the triangle is $aH/2=\sqrt3a^2/4$, while the <circle> has <area> $\pi r^2=\pi a^2/12$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathcal A_{\rm circle}}{\mathcal A_{\rm triangle}}
=\frac{\pi}{3\sqrt3}=\frac{\pi\sqrt3}{9}\approx0.604600.}
$$
The side <length> cancels, as required for a dimensionless ratio between similar configurations.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Put $u=\sin\theta$ and move all terms to one side. The <polynomial> factors as
$$
u^3-u^2-\frac u4+\frac14=(u-1)\left(u-\frac12\right)\left(u+\frac12\right).
$$
The root $u=1$ yields the already supplied $\theta=\pi/2$. In the stated <interval>, $u=1/2$ gives $\pi/6,5\pi/6$, while $u=-1/2$ gives $-\pi/6,-5\pi/6$. Thus all the other solutions are
$$
\boxed{\theta=-\frac{5\pi}{6},\ -\frac\pi6,\ \frac\pi6,\ \frac{5\pi}{6}.}
$$
There are no endpoint solutions because $\sin(\pm\pi)=0$ is not one of the three roots.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

For negative real $x$, the angle $u=\pi e^x$ lies strictly between $0$ and $\pi$. On that <interval>, $\sin u=1/\sqrt2$ has exactly the two solutions $u=\pi/4$ and $u=3\pi/4$. Dividing by $\pi$ and applying the <natural logarithm> yields
$$
\boxed{x=\ln\frac14=-\ln4,\qquad x=\ln\frac34.}
$$
Both values are negative, and the restriction $0<u<\pi$ excludes all further periodic <sine> branches.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

The integrand is a <continuous function>, so the <fundamental theorem of calculus> differentiates the upper limit directly:
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}\int_0^x e^{-y^2}\sin y\,dy=e^{-x^2}\sin x.}
$$
No explicit <antiderivative> is needed.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

For a real-valued expression, $\ln x>0$ requires $x>1$. Applying the <chain rule> twice gives
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}\ln(\ln x)=\frac1{\ln x}\frac1x=\frac1{x\ln x},\qquad x>1.}
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Change the summation index to $j=n+1$. Then $j$ runs from $-99$ to $100$. Each term $j^3$ for $1\leq j\leq99$ cancels its partner $(-j)^3$, and the zero term contributes nothing. The sole unpaired term is $100^3$, so
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=-100}^{99}(n+1)^3=10^6.}
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Since $(1/2)^{3k}=(1/8)^k$, this is a <geometric series> of first term $1$ and common ratio $1/8$. Its sum is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{1-1/8}=\frac87.}
$$

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The cubic <Taylor polynomial> about $a$ is
$$
\boxed{f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2+\frac{f^{(3)}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3.}
$$
If $f$ has a continuous fourth <derivative> near $a$, <Taylor's theorem> gives $f(x)$ equal to this <polynomial> plus $O((x-a)^4)$. With continuous <derivatives> only through third order, the corresponding remainder is $o((x-a)^3)$. An equality with an infinite <Taylor series> would require additional convergence information, rather than just the existence of these four terms.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Factor out $9$ and use the <binomial series> with exponent $-1/2$:
$$
(x^2+9)^{-1/2}=\frac13\left(1+\frac{x^2}{9}\right)^{-1/2}
=\frac13\left[1-\frac12\frac{x^2}{9}+O(x^4)\right].
$$
Thus the expansion through cubic order is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{\sqrt{x^2+9}}=\frac13-\frac{x^2}{54}+O(x^4).}
$$
The <coefficients> of $x$ and $x^3$ vanish because the <function> is an <even function>. The underlying binomial expansion converges for $|x|<3$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Near zero the logarithm's argument is positive, so $\ln[(2+x)^3]=3\ln(2+x)=3\ln2+3\ln(1+x/2)$. Use the <Maclaurin series> of the <natural logarithm>:
$$
\boxed{\ln[(2+x)^3]=3\ln2+\frac32x-\frac38x^2+\frac18x^3+O(x^4).}
$$
The local calculation uses $x>-2$ for the real logarithm; the <power series> converges for $|x|<2$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use $\sin x=x-x^3/6+O(x^5)$ inside the <exponential series>:
$$
e^{\sin x}=1+\left(x-\frac{x^3}{6}\right)+\frac{x^2}{2}
+\frac{x^3}{6}+O(x^4).
$$
The two cubic contributions cancel, leaving
$$
\boxed{e^{\sin x}=1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^4).}
$$
Thus the <coefficient> of $x^3$ is zero, even though the <function> itself is not an <even function>.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\bar s>0$ for the <mean> in minutes. An <exponential distribution> with that <mean> has rate $1/\bar s$, so its <probability density function> is
$$
\boxed{f_S(s)=\begin{cases}\bar s^{-1}e^{-s/\bar s},&s\geq0,\\0,&s<0.\end{cases}}
$$
The density has units of inverse minutes, ensuring that its integral is a dimensionless <probability>.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $s$ on the horizontal axis and $t$ on the vertical axis, both nonnegative. There is no interruption when the noise occurs after the scheduled ending, namely **$t>s$**. This is the part of the first quadrant above the diagonal $t=s$. Equality has <probability> zero, so assigning the boundary either way does not affect the calculation.

The same original diagram below also marks the region used in part (e); its lower boundary offset is an illustrative positive choice of $r_0$.

\Image[paper-1-exponential-regions.png]
{title=The no-interruption region above the diagonal and the region of positive remaining time exceeding a chosen threshold}

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a small rectangle of side lengths $ds,dt$, <independence> makes its <probability> approximately $f_S(s)ds\,f_T(t)dt$. Thus the <joint probability density function> is the product
$$
f_{S,T}(s,t)=\frac1{\bar s\bar t}e^{-s/\bar s-t/\bar t},\qquad s,t\geq0.
$$
Adding the <probabilities> of disjoint small rectangles covering a <region of integration>, and taking their sizes to zero, gives the <double integral> of this product over that region. Without <independence>, the two <marginal distributions> alone would not determine the <joint probability density function>.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Integrate over the region $0\leq s<t$:
$$
P(T>S)=\int_0^\infty\frac{e^{-s/\bar s}}{\bar s}
\left[\int_s^\infty\frac{e^{-t/\bar t}}{\bar t}dt\right]ds
=\frac1{\bar s}\int_0^\infty e^{-s(1/\bar s+1/\bar t)}ds.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{P(\text{no interruption})=\frac{\bar t}{\bar s+\bar t}.}
$$
This is also the two-clock case of <competing exponential clocks>. A larger <mean> time to noise increases this <probability>, while a larger <mean> service <length> decreases it.

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

On interrupted outcomes, $r=S-T$; on the other outcomes the sentinel value is negative and cannot exceed $r_0>0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\{r>r_0\}=\{s>t+r_0,\quad t\geq0\}.}
$$
On the diagram in part (b), this is the first-quadrant region below $t=s-r_0$, starting at the horizontal intercept $s=r_0$. It lies strictly inside the interruption region $t<s$.

= f
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Use the lower limit $s=t+r_0$ for the inner integral:
$$
P(r>r_0)=\int_0^\infty\frac{e^{-t/\bar t}}{\bar t}
\left[\int_{t+r_0}^\infty\frac{e^{-s/\bar s}}{\bar s}ds\right]dt
=\frac{e^{-r_0/\bar s}}{\bar t}\int_0^\infty e^{-t(1/\bar s+1/\bar t)}dt.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{P(r>r_0)=\frac{\bar s}{\bar s+\bar t}e^{-r_0/\bar s},\qquad r_0>0.}
$$
The prefactor is the <probability> that interruption occurs; the remaining exponential is its conditional positive tail. This is the <positive difference of independent exponential waiting times>. The exponent is dimensionless because both $r_0$ and $\bar s$ are measured in minutes.

= g
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

As $r_0\to+\infty$, the exponential factor tends to zero, so **$P(r>r_0)\to0$**. Requiring an arbitrarily large amount of remaining time must have vanishing <probability> for this finite-mean distribution.

= ii
{parent=g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

When $\bar s=\bar t$, the prefactor is $1/2$. Therefore **$P(r>r_0)\to1/2$ as $r_0\downarrow0$**. The clocks then have identical independent continuous laws, so either is equally likely to finish first. A tie has <probability> zero. The limit is the interruption <probability>, rather than one, because non-interrupted outcomes retain the negative sentinel value.

= h
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

The time already elapsed since the noise is $32$ minutes. Conditional on a particular noise time $T=t$ and on still being in progress, <memorylessness of the exponential distribution> gives
$$
P(S-t-32>u\mid S>t+32)=e^{-u/\bar s},\qquad u\geq0.
$$
This law does not depend on $t$. Averaging over the <conditional distribution> of $T$ therefore leaves the same remaining-time law. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[m]=\bar s\ \text{minutes}.}
$$
The elapsed $32$ minutes are not subtracted from the original <mean>; the exponential residual lifetime has the same <mean> after any elapsed duration.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the two-by-two <matrix>, take the product of the diagonal entries minus the product of the off-diagonal entries:
$$
\boxed{\det A=5\cdot5-(-3)(-3)=16.}
$$
In particular, $A$ is an <invertible matrix>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Interchange the diagonal entries, negate the off-diagonal entries, and divide by the nonzero <determinant>. The <matrix inverse> is
$$
\boxed{A^{-1}=\frac1{16}\begin{pmatrix}5&3\\3&5\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication by $A$ gives the <identity matrix>, since each diagonal product sum is $25-9=16$ and each off-diagonal product sum is zero.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <characteristic polynomial> is
$$
\det(A-\lambda I)=(5-\lambda)^2-9=(\lambda-2)(\lambda-8).
$$
For $\lambda=2$, the <eigenvector> equation reduces to $x=y$; for $\lambda=8$, it reduces to $x=-y$. Choose the following <unit vectors> consistently for the remaining parts:
$$
\boxed{\lambda_1=2,\quad x_1=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix};\qquad
\lambda_2=8,\quad x_2=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Each <eigenspace> consists of all <scalar> multiples of its displayed <vector>, including zero; an <eigenvector> itself must be nonzero.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The chosen <eigenvectors> have <dot product>
$$
\boxed{x_1\cdot x_2=\frac{1-1}{2}=0.}
$$
They are therefore <orthogonal>, and their unit lengths make them an <orthonormal eigenbasis>. This agrees with the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices>, since $A$ is real symmetric and its two <eigenvalues> are distinct.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the <quadratic form> out directly:
$$
(x\ y)A\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix}
=x(5x-3y)+y(-3x+5y)=5x^2-6xy+5y^2.
$$
Thus **$b=5$, $c=-6$ and $d=5$**. Both <eigenvalues> are positive, so this is a <positive-definite quadratic form>; its positive unit <level set> is an <ellipse>. The next part exhibits its principal axes explicitly.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $Q=(x_1\ x_2)$ for the normalized <eigenvectors> already chosen. Then $Q^TQ=I$, $(x,y)^T=Q(x',y')^T$, and <orthogonal diagonalization> gives
$$
Q^TAQ=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&8\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\boxed{2(x')^2+8(y')^2=1.}
$$
The <ellipse> has semi-axis lengths $1/\sqrt2$ and $1/\sqrt8$ along $x_1$ and $x_2$, respectively. Rescale $x''=\sqrt2x'$ and $y''=\sqrt8y'$ to obtain the <unit circle> $(x'')^2+(y'')^2=1$. Since $(x',y')^T=Q^T(x,y)^T$,
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}x''\\y''\end{pmatrix}
=B\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}\sqrt2&0\\0&\sqrt8\end{pmatrix}Q^T
=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\2&-2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The identity $B^TB=A$ checks the rescaling directly. Swapping or reversing <eigenvectors> gives other valid coordinate matrices; the displayed one follows the explicit choices in part (a).

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the real <Fourier series> convention for <period> $2\pi$,
$$
\boxed{y(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}[a_n\cos(nx)+b_n\sin(nx)],}
$$
where the <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
a_n=\frac1\pi\int_0^{2\pi}y(x)\cos(nx)dx\quad(n\geq0),\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_0^{2\pi}y(x)\sin(nx)dx\quad(n\geq1).
$$
These formulas apply to integrable periodic <functions>. Writing equality at each point requires appropriate convergence hypotheses; the periodic solution sought below is <smooth>, so its series causes no such difficulty.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate the <Fourier series> term by term and collect <cosine> and <sine> terms. Substitution into the equation gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
[(a_n+\sqrt3\,n b_n)\cos(nx)+(b_n-\sqrt3\,n a_n)\sin(nx)]
=4\sin x.}
$$
For a <smooth> periodic solution, <differentiation> of the series is justified. Alternatively, multiply the <differential equation> by each trigonometric mode and integrate by parts; periodicity cancels the boundary terms and gives the same <coefficient> constraints without first differentiating an infinite sum.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<Fourier orthogonality> isolates a <coefficient>. Multiplication by $\sin(mx)$ and <integration> over $[0,2\pi]$ annihilates the constant, all <cosine> terms and all <sine> terms except the one with $n=m$, whose squared integral is $\pi$. Thus the corresponding <coefficients> on the two sides must agree for each positive integer $m$.

Multiplication by $\cos(mx)$ works in the same way, while <integration> against the constant mode extracts $a_0$. This is the uniqueness of the <Fourier coefficients>; it does not follow from comparing a few point values of the series.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The constant <coefficient> is $a_0=0$. For every $n\geq1$ the two mode equations are
$$
a_n+\sqrt3\,n b_n=0,\qquad b_n-\sqrt3\,n a_n=4\delta_{n1},
$$
where $\delta_{n1}$ is the <Kronecker delta>. The first equation gives $a_n=-\sqrt3\,n b_n$, so the second becomes $(1+3n^2)b_n=4\delta_{n1}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{a_0=0,\qquad a_1=-\sqrt3,\quad b_1=1,\qquad a_n=b_n=0\ (n\geq2).}
$$
No other modes survive. This is the single-frequency case of the <Fourier solution of a first-order equation with periodic forcing>.

= e
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Using the <sine addition formula>, combine the surviving modes:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sin x-\sqrt3\cos x=2\sin(x-\pi/3).}
$$
It has <amplitude> $2$ and a <phase> delay $\pi/3$. The full <general solution> would add $Ce^{-x/\sqrt3}$, but this exponential is periodic only for $C=0$, so the displayed periodic solution is unique.

= f
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The <curve> starts at $y(0)=-\sqrt3$ and initially rises. It crosses zero at $x=\pi/3$ and $4\pi/3$, attains its maximum at **$(5\pi/6,2)$** and minimum at **$(11\pi/6,-2)$**, then returns to $y(2\pi)=-\sqrt3$. These follow either from the shifted <sine> form or from $y'=2\cos(x-\pi/3)$.

\Image[paper-1-periodic-response.png]
{title=The unique periodic sinusoidal response, with its maximum, minimum and zero crossings}

= g
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

In the convention with constant term $a_0/2$, <Parseval's identity> is
$$
\int_0^{2\pi}y(x)^2dx=\pi\left[\frac{a_0^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(a_n^2+b_n^2)\right].
$$
Only the first harmonic contributes here, so
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{2\pi}y(x)^2dx=\pi[(-\sqrt3)^2+1^2]=4\pi.}
$$

= h
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

Square the two-term form and integrate directly:
$$
\int_0^{2\pi}(\sin x-\sqrt3\cos x)^2dx
=\int_0^{2\pi}\sin^2x\,dx+3\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^2x\,dx
-2\sqrt3\int_0^{2\pi}\sin x\cos x\,dx.
$$
The first two integrals are each $\pi$ before their prefactors, while the cross term is zero because its integrand is $\tfrac12\sin2x$. Thus the result is again **$4\pi$**, agreeing with <Parseval's identity>.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <vector triple product identity> gives
$$
\boxed{a\times(b\times c)=(a\cdot c)b-(a\cdot b)c.}
$$
It is the requested <linear combination> of $b$ and $c$. The notation $\wedge$ in this question denotes the three-dimensional <cross product>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Expand the same double <cross product> in two ways. First expanding with $c\times d$ as the inner <cross product> gives
$$
(a\times b)\times(c\times d)
=[(a\times b)\cdot d]c-[(a\times b)\cdot c]d
=[a,b,d]c-[a,b,c]d.
$$
Alternatively, use $(a\times b)\times w=(a\cdot w)b-(b\cdot w)a$ with $w=c\times d$ to get
$$
(a\times b)\times(c\times d)=[a,c,d]b-[b,c,d]a.
$$
Equating these expressions and moving every term to one side proves
$$
[b,c,d]a-[a,c,d]b+[a,b,d]c-[a,b,c]d=0.
$$
This <four-vector scalar triple product identity> is valid without assuming that any three of the four <vectors> are independent.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At an intersection, the two <position> <vectors> agree, so $a-b=tn-sm$. The <scalar triple product> with $m,n$ is therefore
$$
[a-b,m,n]=t[n,m,n]-s[m,m,n]=\boxed{0},
$$
since a <determinant> with repeated columns vanishes. Geometrically the displacement of the two anchor points must lie in the same <plane> as the two <line> directions.

This is a necessary condition. If $m,n$ are not parallel, it is also sufficient, because their span is exactly the <plane> perpendicular to $m\times n$. For parallel directions the triple product vanishes automatically, even for distinct <lines>, so the condition alone cannot guarantee intersection.

= d
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $D=[a,m,n]\ne0$ and rewrite the intersection equation as $b=a+sm-tn$. Take <scalar triple products> with $a$ and $n$:
$$
[a,b,n]=[a,a+sm-tn,n]=sD.
$$
Taking the triple product with $a$ and $m$ instead gives
$$
[a,b,m]=[a,a+sm-tn,m]=-t[a,n,m]=tD.
$$
Thus the <intersection parameters from scalar triple products> are
$$
\boxed{s=\frac{[a,b,n]}{[a,m,n]},\qquad t=\frac{[a,b,m]}{[a,m,n]}.}
$$
The assumed nonzero denominator makes these expressions valid and guarantees nonparallel directions. The resulting point is $r=a+sm=b+tn$; these formulas are conditional on the intersection assumed in the question.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Set $v=y'$ to reduce the <second-order linear differential equation> to $xv'+v=0$. Equivalently, $(xv)'=0$, so $v=C_1/x$ on any <interval> excluding zero. The <derivative> condition at $x=1$ gives $C_1=2$.

Integrating on the positive <interval> containing the initial point yields $y=2\ln x+C_2$. The value $y(1)=-1$ then fixes $C_2=-1$, giving
$$
\boxed{y(x)=2\ln x-1,\qquad x>0.}
$$
Its <derivative> is $2/x$, and direct substitution verifies the equation. The selected solution diverges as $x\downarrow0$ and cannot extend through zero as a finite-valued <function>.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Construct the <scalar potential> $F(x,y)=ye^{x^2}-x^2$. Its differential is
$$
dF=2x(ye^{x^2}-1)dx+e^{x^2}dy,
$$
which proves exactness directly. Multiplying the <differential equation> by $e^{x^2}$ shows that a solution satisfies $dF/dx=0$, so $ye^{x^2}-x^2=C$. The initial value at zero gives $C=-1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=(x^2-1)e^{-x^2},\qquad x\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The factor $e^{x^2}$ is also an <integrating factor> for the associated first-order linear equation.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <complementary function> is $C\cos2x+D\sin2x$. A <particular solution> is $(\sin x+\cos x)/3$, since applying $d^2/dx^2+4$ to either <sine> or <cosine> multiplies it by $3$. Thus
$$
y=C\cos2x+D\sin2x+\frac13(\sin x+\cos x).
$$
At zero, $C+1/3=3$, giving $C=8/3$. At $x=\pi/4$, the <cosine> term vanishes and the particular term is $\sqrt2/3$, so $D=-\sqrt2/3$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{8\cos2x-\sqrt2\sin2x+\sin x+\cos x}{3}.}
$$
The two boundary conditions determine the two constants uniquely because their evaluation matrix on $\cos2x,\sin2x$ is the identity.

