= Solution
{parent=i}

The point has positive <real part> and negative <imaginary part>. Its <complex modulus> is $\sqrt{1+3}=2$, and its principal <complex argument> is $-\pi/3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{|z|=2,\qquad\arg z=-\frac\pi3+2\pi k,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use <Euler's formula> to evaluate the two exponentials: $e^{i\pi/2}=i$ and $\sqrt2e^{i\pi/4}=1+i$. Thus $z=1+2i$ lies in the first quadrant, giving
$$
\boxed{|z|=\sqrt5,\qquad\arg z=\arctan(2)+2\pi k,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Its principal argument is approximately $1.10715$ radians.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $1+i=\sqrt2e^{i\pi/4}$ and multiply the phases:
$$
z=\sqrt2e^{i(\pi/4+\pi/6)}=\sqrt2e^{5\pi i/12}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{|z|=\sqrt2,\qquad\arg z=\frac{5\pi}{12}+2\pi k,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The displayed representative already lies in the principal argument <interval>.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set $w=z^3$. Then $w^2+w+1=0$ gives $w=e^{2\pi i/3}$ or $e^{-2\pi i/3}$. Taking all three cube roots of each yields six distinct solutions.

Equivalently, multiply the original <polynomial> by $z^3-1$ to obtain $z^9-1$. The original roots are exactly the ninth <roots of unity> that are not cube <roots of unity>: a cube root would give $1+1+1=3$, not zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{z=e^{2\pi i k/9},\qquad k\in\{1,2,4,5,7,8\}.}
$$
All six have modulus one, and their distinct arguments show that no roots have been repeated or omitted from the degree-six <polynomial>.

= 18
