= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

If the form is $dF$ for a <scalar> <function> with continuous second <partial derivatives>, then $p=F_x$ and $q=F_y$. Equality of the mixed <partial derivatives> therefore requires
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial p}{\partial y}=\frac{\partial q}{\partial x}.}
$$
This is necessary for an <exact differential>. For continuously <differentiable> <coefficients> on a simply connected open domain it is also sufficient; on a domain with holes, the equality alone need not give a single-valued global potential.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the printed <coefficients>, $p=2\sin x\cosh y$ and $q=\sin^2x\sinh y$. Their cross <derivatives> are
$$
p_y=2\sin x\sinh y,\qquad q_x=2\sin x\cos x\sinh y.
$$
They are not equal as <functions>; their difference is $2\sin x(1-\cos x)\sinh y$. Hence **the form is not exact**. Agreement on certain <lines> or isolated points is insufficient, because exactness requires the <derivative> identity throughout an open domain.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $w=x+iy$. Then $y-ix=-iw$, and the complex trigonometric identity $\sin(-iw)=-i\sinh w$ gives
$$
\sinh(x+iy)dx-\sin(y-ix)dy
=\sinh w(dx+i\,dy)=d[\cosh(x+iy)].
$$
Thus **the form is exact**, with complex-valued <scalar potential> $F(x,y)=\cosh(x+iy)$. Differentiating that potential with respect to the two real variables checks both <coefficients> individually.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Away from zeros of $\sin x$, an <integrating factor for a differential one-form> is $\mu(x)=1/\sin x$. Multiplication converts the form to
$$
(\cot x+y)dx+xdy=d[\ln|\sin x|+xy].
$$
Therefore the implicit solution family is $xy+\ln|\sin x|=C$. On each graph <interval> between consecutive multiples of $\pi$, this becomes
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{C-\ln|\sin x|}{x},\qquad k\pi<x<(k+1)\pi,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
No such open <interval> contains zero. At $x=k\pi$, the undivided graph equation would read $\cos(k\pi)=0$, which is impossible for a finite <derivative>; hence no <differentiable> graph extends across one of these points.

If solutions are instead understood as arbitrary parametrized integral <curves> of the <differential form>, the vertical <lines> **$x=k\pi$** are additional solutions: along them $dx=0$ and $x\sin x\,dy=0$. They were lost on division by $\sin x$ and are not graphs $y(x)$ over an <interval>.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

View $a=a(b,c)$ with $b=b(d,e)$ and $c=c(d,e)$. Hold $e$ fixed and apply the <multivariable chain rule>:
$$
 da=\left(\frac{\partial a}{\partial b}\right)_c db
 +\left(\frac{\partial a}{\partial c}\right)_b dc.
$$
Dividing the changes along the fixed-$e$ variation by $dd$ gives exactly the two terms in the question. Thus the simpler expression is
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial a}{\partial d}\right)_e.}
$$
The subscripts specify which coordinate is held fixed and must be retained.

