= Solution
{parent=a}

The <divergence> is
$$
\nabla\cdot F=\partial_x(y^2)+\partial_y(e^{xz})+\partial_z(e^{xy})=0.
$$
For any closed piecewise <smooth> surface bounding a <volume>, use the <divergence theorem> with the outward orientation:
$$
\boxed{\int_SF\cdot dS=\int_V\nabla\cdot F\,dV=0.}
$$
Reversing the orientation still gives zero. The field is <smooth> everywhere, so there is no hidden singular source inside the surface.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose the <plane> normal to be $+\widehat y$. Only the second field component contributes, and on $y=0$ it is $e^{-(x^2+z^2)}$. Thus the <surface integral> factors into two <Gaussian integrals>:
$$
\boxed{\int_SF\cdot dS
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-x^2-z^2}\,dx\,dz
=(\sqrt\pi)^2=\pi.}
$$
The original question does not specify an orientation; choosing $-\widehat y$ instead gives **$-\pi$**.

The printed first component $\sin y/y$ is undefined on the entire <integration> <plane>. Its removable continuous extension there is $1$, since $\lim_{y\to0}\sin y/y=1$. The stated <flux> uses this natural extension; in fact the normal component alone is already well-defined and integrable on the <plane>, so the first component does not affect either oriented value.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take the <sphere>'s outward normal. Since $F=k(x,y,z)$ with constant $k$, its <divergence> is $3k$. The <divergence theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_SF\cdot dS=3k\,\frac{4\pi r^3}{3}=4\pi kr^3.}
$$
The translated <centre> does not enter the answer, because the <divergence> is constant. Equivalently the constant field produced by translating coordinates has zero net <flux> through a closed <sphere>. The inward orientation would negate the result.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Choose the upward orientation of the paraboloid cap. Its boundary is the <circle> $x^2+y^2=4$ at $z=1$, oriented counterclockwise as viewed from above. By <Stokes theorem>,
$$
\int_S(\nabla\times A)\cdot dS=\oint_{\partial S}A\cdot dr.
$$
On the boundary, $dz=0$ and $A\cdot dr=y\,dx-3xy\,dy$. Parametrize by $x=2\cos\theta$, $y=2\sin\theta$, $0\leq\theta\leq2\pi$. Then
$$
\oint_{\partial S}A\cdot dr
=\int_0^{2\pi}[-4\sin^2\theta-24\cos^2\theta\sin\theta]d\theta
=\boxed{-4\pi}.
$$
The second term integrates to zero over a full <period>, while the first contributes $-4\pi$. Equivalently, replace the cap by the upward disk at $z=1$: there $(\nabla\times A)_z=-3y-1$, whose integral is $-4\pi$ by symmetry.

An orientation is not stated in the original question. The displayed result uses the upward normal, also the outward normal on the curved top of the enclosed cap; the opposite orientation gives **$4\pi$**.

