= Solution
{parent=i}

Set $a=\pi/2$ for this part. In the <interval> $(-\pi,\pi)$ the special points are $-a,0,a$. Elsewhere $f_0=\sin(\tan x)$ is a composition of <smooth> <functions>.

At zero, $\tan x=x+O(x^3)$ and therefore $\sin(\tan x)=x+O(x^3)$. Its assigned value is zero, so the <function> is continuous and <differentiable> there, with $f_0'(0)=1$.

At either tangent pole $\pm a$, the bounded <sine> oscillates without a limit. For an explicit check at $a$, let $x_j=\arctan(2\pi j+\pi/2)$ and $u_j=\arctan(2\pi j+3\pi/2)$. Both approach $a$ from below, but the <function> values are respectively $1$ and $-1$. Negating these sequences gives the same obstruction at $-a$. Assigning the value zero at the poles cannot repair either limit.

Thus **$f_0$ is discontinuous and nondifferentiable exactly at $x=\pm\pi/2$**, and is <smooth> elsewhere in the specified <interval>.

\Image[paper-1-oscillation-n0.png]
{title=Undamped tangent-phase oscillations with assigned zero values at both poles and an ordinary differentiable crossing at zero}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $-a$, the prefactor $(x-a)/x$ tends to $2$, so the nonvanishing <amplitude> leaves the oscillatory discontinuity intact. At zero, use $\sin(\tan x)=x+O(x^3)$ to obtain
$$
f_1(x)=\left(1-\frac ax\right)[x+O(x^3)]
=-a+x+O(x^2).
$$
The limit is $-a$, whereas the assigned value is zero. This is a <removable discontinuity>, not a continuous crossing.

At $a$, write $x=a+h$. The estimate
$$
|f_1(a+h)|\leq\frac{|h|}{|a+h|}\longrightarrow0
$$
proves <continuity> at the assigned zero. But its <difference quotient> is
$$
\frac{f_1(a+h)-f_1(a)}h=\frac{\sin(\tan(a+h))}{a+h}.
$$
The sequences from part (i) make this approach $1/a$ and $-1/a$, so the <derivative> does not exist. This illustrates the distinct <continuity and differentiability thresholds for a damped oscillation>.

Therefore **the discontinuities are $x=-\pi/2$ and $x=0$; the nondifferentiability points are $x=-\pi/2,0,\pi/2$**. The <function> is <smooth> elsewhere.

\Image[paper-1-oscillation-n1.png]
{title=Linear suppression of the positive-pole oscillations, with a removable discontinuity at zero}

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $-a$, the squared prefactor tends to $4$, so the oscillatory discontinuity again remains. Near zero,
$$
f_2(x)=\left(1-\frac{2a}{x}+\frac{a^2}{x^2}\right)[x+O(x^3)]
=\frac{a^2}{x}-2a+O(x).
$$
It is unbounded near zero, despite its assigned zero value there, so it is neither continuous nor <differentiable> at zero.

At $a$, put $x=a+h$. Then
$$
|f_2(a+h)|\leq\frac{h^2}{(a+h)^2},\qquad
\left|\frac{f_2(a+h)-f_2(a)}h\right|\leq\frac{|h|}{(a+h)^2}\longrightarrow0.
$$
Thus **$f_2$ is continuous and <differentiable> at $x=\pi/2$, with $f_2'(\pi/2)=0$**. After translating the point to zero, the estimate shows how <quadratic damping restores differentiability at the origin>. The only discontinuities and nondifferentiability points are **$x=-\pi/2$ and $x=0$**; the <function> is <smooth> away from these two points and $\pi/2$. At $\pi/2$, its <derivative> exists but is not continuous.

Indeed, for $x\ne a$ near $a$, put $B(x)=(x-a)^2/x^2$. Then $f_2'=B'\sin(\tan x)+B\sec^2x\cos(\tan x)$. Here $B'\to0$ and $B\sec^2x\to1/a^2$, so the <derivative> retains oscillations. Existence of the <derivative> at a point does not require <continuity> of nearby <derivatives>.

\Image[paper-1-oscillation-n2.png]
{title=Quadratic suppression giving a zero derivative at the positive pole, and an unbounded discontinuity at zero}

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $u=x^{-5}$, which tends to zero as $|x|\to\infty$. Factor the denominator and use the <cosecant expansion near zero>:
$$
g(x)=x^2\frac{u}{\sin u},\qquad
\frac{u}{\sin u}=\left(1-\frac{u^2}{6}+\frac{u^4}{120}+O(u^6)\right)^{-1}
=1+\frac{u^2}{6}+\frac{7u^4}{360}+O(u^6).
$$
Substitution gives the first two nonzero terms and the requested omitted order:
$$
\boxed{g(x)=x^2+\frac1{6x^8}+O(x^{-18}),\qquad |x|\to\infty.}
$$
More precisely the next term is $7/(360x^{18})$, followed by $O(x^{-28})$. The outside factor $x^2$ is essential when converting the remainder order from powers of $u$ to powers of $x$.
